    "case_id": "487",
    "case_summary": "A 49-year-old man of Sudanese descent presented with a 5-day history of progressive painful swelling of the face, particularly affecting the eyelids, periorbital tissues, and hands. He took no drugs and had no allergies. He had traveled to Sudan 6 months previously. Over the next 7 days, he became increasingly drowsy and weak until he was unable to walk independently.\n\nExamination revealed striking infiltration of the eyelids and periorbital skin with multiple firm tumorous swellings of the forehead and temples. The skin of the forehead, neck, shoulders, and anterior chest had a diffusely rippled appearance. The hands and fingers were swollen with scattered focal purpuric infarcts.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:\n  - C-reactive protein: 43 mg/L (normal <10 mg/L)\n  - No eosinophilia\n  - Normal complement concentrations\n\n- Imaging Studies:\n  - Chest radiograph: normal\n\n- Pathological Findings:\n  - Initial superficial punch biopsy (upper arm): showed only oedema\n  - Deep incisional biopsy (nodule in front of left ear):\n    * Dermal oedema and scattered histiocytes\n    * Widespread fibrinoid exudates within deeper tissues\n    * Granulomatous inflammation with intense neutrophilic abscesses in subcutaneous fat\n    * Initial mucin stains (PAS, Alcian blue): negative\n    * Hale's colloidal iron staining: showed deep dermal mucin\n\n- Images:\n  Figure 1: Dramatic infiltration of the eyelids and periorbital skin with multiple firm tumorous swellings of the forehead and temples",
